---
/**
 * SpecMeta (components.md §7) — the review rail's spec block.
 *
 * Every row below maps to a field that already exists in the content model.
 * The reference mockup also showed a tracklist, BPM and a staff credit; none
 * of those exist here, so none of them are here. Runtime WAS on that list
 * too, but joined the row set 2026-09-09 once the album schema grew a
 * `duration` field (see the Runtime row below) — a tracklist and BPM still
 * have no backing data, so they remain absent. An optional field with no
 * value removes its ROW — it never renders a dash or a blank, because a spec
 * sheet full of placeholders reads as a broken page (R-4).
 *
 * `mbid` is deliberately not surfaced: it is an internal identifier and
 * means nothing to a reader.
 *
 * ROW SET AND ORDER (2026-09-09, decision-maker request): Title / Artist /
 * Genre / Label / Runtime / Tags / Release / Reviewed — three groups, each
 * internally ordered by how directly it identifies the record:
 *   1. IDENTITY  — Title, Artist: what the record is and who made it.
 *   2. CATEGORY  — Genre, Label, Runtime, Tags: how it is classified (Genre,
 *      Tags) or a plain fact about it (Label, Runtime). Genre sits right
 *      under Artist because it is an album property exactly like Artist is
 *      (both describe the RECORD, not this magazine's relationship to it) —
 *      the same reasoning that used to put Genre near the bottom is reversed
 *      here on the decision-maker's explicit instruction. Runtime (added
 *      2026-09-09, once the album schema grew a `duration` field) sits right
 *      after Label rather than at either end of this group: like Label, it
 *      is a single plain fact with no link out to the archive (unlike Genre
 *      and Tags, which both wrap their value in `searchLink` below) — the
 *      two unlinked facts sit together, and the two linked/classifying
 *      fields (Genre above them, Tags after) bracket the pair rather than
 *      interleaving with it.
 *   3. DATES     — Release, Reviewed: adjacent, on purpose. They used to sit
 *      apart (Release up top, Reviewed at the foot) with Genre/Label/Tags
 *      between them, which hid the one relationship a reader most wants from
 *      a review-of-record: how long after release did this magazine get to
 *      it. Two dates side by side answer that in one glance ("released
 *      March, reviewed August") without arithmetic, and that gap is
 *      editorially meaningful for this publication (it is the whole reason
 *      "Reviewed" exists as a field distinct from "Release" in the first
 *      place) — so the two rows are the closing pair, in chronological order.
 *
 * THE TITLE ROW DUPLICATES THE PAGE'S OWN <h1>, DELIBERATELY. The reader
 * already has the album's name from the hero two lines above the rail — so
 * on its face this row repeats itself for free. It stays for the same reason
 * Release already (partially) repeats the hero's own date and Genre already
 * repeats the hero's own bucket label before this row existed at all (see
 * reviews/[slug].astro's hero-sub, which has always carried
 * "{artists} · {bucket} · {release YEAR}" alongside this block — YEAR only
 * since 2026-09-09, this row's own Release value keeps the full date, so the
 * two no longer say exactly the same thing, but they still describe the same
 * fact at different granularities): the spec block was never a set of facts
 * the hero omits, it is a COMPLETE, SELF-
 * CONTAINED fact sheet a reader can scan without having read the hero at
 * all — a rail is exactly the kind of surface a reader's eye can land on
 * first (it is visually first on narrow screens, per the reskin's
 * `order: -1` on ScoreMark) or return to later without rereading the title
 * block. Treating Title as one more row of that same sheet, rather than a
 * special case that gets left out, keeps the block's own rule ("every fact
 * about the record lives here") intact instead of carving an exception into
 * it. What DOES change to keep the repetition from reading as a mistake
 * rather than a design: the row's value takes the same weight and size as
 * every other row's value (`dd`'s ordinary sans/14px), never the h1's
 * display face — it reads as data, not as a second headline. Subtitle
 * (album schema's optional `subtitle`, 2026-09-09) appends in parentheses on
 * its OWN LINE when present (2026-09-10, decision-maker request: "제목 하고
 * 부제 사이에 줄바꿈 했으면좋겠어" — the title and its qualifier used to run
 * together on one line, "Lost Weekend (The 3rd Studio Album)", which read as
 * one long title rather than a title plus a qualifier). The row shows the
 * title alone, one line, when subtitle is absent — per the same R-4 "omit
 * the part with nothing to say" rule the whole block already follows for
 * Label/Tags, and the reason the line break below is INSIDE the
 * `subtitle &&` guard rather than always printed: a title with no subtitle
 * must stay a single line, never a title line followed by an empty one.
 *
 * THE PARENTHESES STAY (left open by the decision-maker, resolved here).
 * Breaking the line already tells a SIGHTED reader "the second line
 * qualifies the first" — but a screen reader reads the two `dd` lines as one
 * run of text with no pause encoded in a `<br>`, so without the parentheses
 * "Lost Weekend The 3rd Studio Album" would sound like one title. Keeping
 * them costs nothing visually (the line break already carries the visual
 * separation) and keeps the two readings — by eye and by ear — in agreement.
 *
 * THE SUBTITLE LINE IS VISUALLY WEAKER THAN THE TITLE (`.subtitle` below,
 * `--muted` in place of `--ink`, size UNCHANGED). Colour rather than a
 * second size step is the same choice ArticleCard's own `.subtitle` already
 * made for its title/subtitle pair — reusing it here keeps "how a subtitle
 * is de-emphasised" one rule on the site, not two. Reusing `--muted` on this
 * block's own `--surface-2` background is also deliberate, not incidental —
 * that exact pair already carries the block's `dt` labels at 4.5:1+
 * (browser.contrast.spec.ts), so this new line introduces no colour
 * combination the contrast suite has not already passed.
 *
 * THE RELEASE ROW LINKS TO THE ARCHIVE, PRE-FILTERED TO ITS YEAR
 * (2026-09-09, decision-maker request). The per-year archive page
 * (`/archive/{year}/`) that a link like this used to point at was retired in
 * the same redesign that added this link — the archive is now one hub page
 * with a query box, not a page per axis value — so the destination is
 * `/archive/?q=<releaseYear>` instead: a plain link (works with JS off, just
 * lands on the unfiltered hub — see archive/index.astro's own intro for why
 * that is the accepted JS-off shape everywhere on this page) that the hub's
 * script pre-fills and pre-filters ON LOAD when JS is on, by reading `?q=`
 * with the exact same code path a typed query or a clicked chip already use
 * (enhance.js — one filter, three ways to trigger it). The value navigated
 * to is the RELEASE year, not the review's publication year, because that
 * is the year printed in this very row — a link that opened a different
 * year than the one the reader clicked would be its own defect. Genre and
 * Tags below link the same way, to their own label text.
 */
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths.ts';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  subtitle?: string;
  artistLinks: { slug: string; name: string }[];
  releaseDateText: string;
  releaseYear: string;
  bucketLabels: { id: string; label: string }[];
  reviewDate: string;
  label?: string;
  /** Running time, e.g. "52:26" (album schema's optional `duration`,
   * 2026-09-09) — already display-ready (ReviewPageData#duration's own doc:
   * validated MM:SS by common.ts#durationSchema, no reformatting here).
   * undefined ⇒ the row is omitted, same R-4 rule as Label/Tags above. */
  duration?: string;
  tags: { slug: string; label: string }[];
}

const { title, subtitle, artistLinks, releaseDateText, releaseYear, bucketLabels, reviewDate, label, duration, tags } = Astro.props;

/** `/archive/?q=<value>` — the one place a static link reaches a pre-filtered
 * search hub (see the intro above). Centralised so Genre and Tags below
 * cannot drift from Release's own construction of the same kind of link. */
const searchLink = (value: string) => withBase(`/archive/?q=${encodeURIComponent(value)}`);
---

<dl class="spec" aria-label="앨범 정보">
  <div class="row">
    <dt>Title</dt>
    {/* Kept on one line, unlike most of this file's markup: Astro preserves
        template whitespace as literal text, and build.dist-matrix.spec.ts
        matches this row by its EXACT text content (`<dd>제목</dd>`, no
        subtitle) — a line break between `{title}` and the closing tag would
        turn that into a false negative on a suite this repo has been burned
        by before ("A와 B를 서로 비교하는 검사는 A와 B가 같이 틀린 걸 못 본다" —
        see other files' notes on the same failure shape). `{subtitle && <br />}`
        and the span below are both skipped entirely when there is no
        subtitle, so the no-subtitle row is untouched by this change. */}
    <dd>{title}{subtitle && <br />}{subtitle && <span class="subtitle">({subtitle})</span>}</dd>
  </div>
  <div class="row">
    <dt>Artist</dt>
    <dd>
      {
        artistLinks.map((artist, i) => (
          <>
            {i > 0 && ', '}
            <a class="artist-link" href={withBase(`/artists/${artist.slug}/`)}>
              {artist.name}
            </a>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </dd>
  </div>
  <div class="row">
    {/* MULTI-GENRE (2026-09-09): an album can belong to more than one
        bucket (album schema §"복수 장르") — every one of them renders here,
        in the same array order the content lists them (no ranking implied,
        same as everywhere else this array is consumed). Singular "Genre"
        label stays even with several values: it names the AXIS, not a count
        (same convention SpecMeta's own Tags row and the archive hub's axis
        titles already use). */}
    <dt>Genre</dt>
    <dd>
      {
        bucketLabels.map((bucket, i) => (
          <>
            {i > 0 && ', '}
            <a class="meta-link" href={searchLink(bucket.label)}>
              {bucket.label}
            </a>
          </>
        ))
      }
    </dd>
  </div>
  {
    label && (
      <div class="row">
        <dt>Label</dt>
        <dd>{label}</dd>
      </div>
    )
  }
  {
    duration && (
      <div class="row">
        <dt>Runtime</dt>
        <dd class="tnum">{duration}</dd>
      </div>
    )
  }
  {
    tags.length > 0 && (
      <div class="row">
        <dt>Tags</dt>
        <dd class="tags">
          {tags.map((tag) => (
            <a class="chip" href={searchLink(tag.label)}>
              {tag.label}
            </a>
          ))}
        </dd>
      </div>
    )
  }
  <div class="row">
    <dt>Release</dt>
    <dd class="tnum"><a class="meta-link" href={searchLink(releaseYear)}>{releaseDateText}</a></dd>
  </div>
  <div class="row">
    <dt>Reviewed</dt>
    <dd><time class="tnum" datetime={reviewDate}>{reviewDate}</time></dd>
  </div>
</dl>

<style>
  .spec {
    margin: 0;
    background: var(--surface-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--r-0);
    padding: var(--s-12);
  }
  .row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 84px 1fr;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    padding: 6px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .row:last-child {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }
  dt {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    line-height: 16px;
    letter-spacing: var(--label-ls);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: var(--fs-14);
    line-height: 20px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--ink);
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* Title row's optional second line (2026-09-10) — same size as the title
     above it, `--muted` in place of `--ink` is the whole weakening. See the
     intro for why colour alone (not a smaller size) matches how every other
     title/subtitle pair on the site is already de-emphasised. */
  .subtitle {
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  /* Links inside a value are inline text, so the 44px target rule does not
     apply (WCAG 2.5.8 inline exception) — they get color + underline. */
  .artist-link,
  .meta-link {
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    transition: text-decoration-thickness var(--t-micro);
  }
  .artist-link:hover,
  .meta-link:hover {
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }
  .tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }
  .chip {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 24px;
    padding: var(--s-4) 6px;
    background: var(--surface-3);
    border-radius: var(--r-0);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    line-height: 16px;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--muted);
    transition: color var(--t-micro);
  }
  .chip:hover {
    color: var(--accent-2-ink);
  }
</style>
